  22:     Fill each blue rectangular frame with teal and extend teal outward from any one-cell border gap.
  23: 
  24:     Input structure
  25:     - The grid has a black background and one foreground color forming 4-connected rectangular frame objects.
  26:     - Each frame's bounding-box border is either complete or has exactly one missing non-corner border pixel.
  27:     - Frame interiors and the outward line from each border gap are black in the input.
  28:     Rule
  29:     - For every frame, recolor all pixels strictly inside its bbox to teal.
  30:     - If the frame has a missing border pixel, recolor that gap and every black pixel from it straight outward, perpendicular to that side, through the grid edge.
  31:     - Keep the blue frame pixels and all other black background pixels unchanged.
  32:     Key insight
  33:     - A missing border pixel is not a defect to repair with blue; it is a doorway that defines the direction of the teal spill from the filled rectangle.
  34:     """
  35:     g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)
  36:     assert g.ndim == 2
  37: 
  38:     bg = get_bg(g)
  39:     assert bg == Color.BLACK
  40:     frame_colors = set(map(int, g.ravel())) - {bg}
  41:     assert len(frame_colors) == 1
  42:     frame_color = next(iter(frame_colors))
  43:     assert frame_color == Color.BLUE
  44: 
  45:     frames = find_components(
  46:         g,
  47:         bg=bg,
  48:         connectivity=4,
  49:         monochromatic=True,
  50:         colors=[frame_color],
  51:         return_format="dict",
  52:     )
  53:     assert frames
  54: 
  55:     out = g.copy()
  56:     h_grid, w_grid = g.shape
  57:     for frame in frames:
